  "schema_version": "2.0",
  "report_markdown": "## Executive Summary\n\n- Trisomy 13 and 18 are more common among parents aged 35 and older.\n- Trisomy is a genetic condition that results in an extra copy of a chromosome.\n- A person with trisomy will have 47 chromosomes instead of 46.\n- Your risk of having a child with a trisomy condition increases after age 35.\n- Trisomy 18 is a life-threatening genetic disorder that impacts about 1 out of every 2000 pregnancies in the U.S.\n- Trisomy 18 occurs in about one out of every 6,000 to 8,000 live births.\n- Trisomy 13 occurs in about one out of every 8,000 to 12,000 live births.\n- Babies with trisomy 18 or 13 disorders usually die by age 1.\n- Joshua has Down syndrome (trisomy 21) and has a friend named Charlie who is one week younger than him.\n- Joshua and Charlie first met when they were only 2 months old.\n- The Down syndrome journey has brought the author many friendships over the last 6.5 years.\n- There is no cure for trisomies 18 and 13 at this time.\n- Many babies born with trisomy 18 and 13 disorders may not live beyond the first few days or weeks of life.\n- Anna has trisomy 13 and is living a good quality of life.\n\n## Findings\n\n### Trisomy Definition and Chromosomal Basis\nTrisomy is a genetic condition that results in an extra copy of a chromosome. Consequently, a person with trisomy will have 47 chromosomes instead of the typical 46.\n\n### Maternal Age and Risk\nTrisomy 13 and 18 are more common among parents aged 35 and older. The risk of having a child with a trisomy condition increases after age 35.\n\n### Prevalence of Trisomy 18 and 13\nTrisomy 18 is a life-threatening genetic disorder that impacts about 1 out of every 2,000 pregnancies in the U.S. However, it occurs in about one out of every 6,000 to 8,000 live births. Trisomy 13 occurs in about one out of every 8,000 to 12,000 live births.\n\n### Prognosis and Survival\nBabies with trisomy 18 or 13 disorders usually die by age 1. Many babies born with these disorders may not live beyond the first few days or weeks of life. There is no cure for trisomies 18 and 13 at this time.\n\n### Individual Cases and Quality of Life\nAnna has trisomy 13 and is living a good quality of life. Joshua has Down syndrome (trisomy 21) and has a friend named Charlie who is one week younger than him. Joshua and Charlie first met when they were only 2 months old. The Down syndrome journey has brought the author many friendships over the last 6.5 years.\n\n### Absence of Identifying Information\nThe provided source excerpts do not contain any information identifying Person A, Person B, the food-related nickname, or the trisomy disorder context described in the task.\n\n## Evidence Status\n\nAll claims listed in the Findings section were accepted by the critic, with evidence spans directly matching the claims. The claim regarding the absence of identifying information (Person A, Person B, food-related nickname) was also accepted, though it was marked as critical. No claims were rejected or marked as unsupported. All accepted claims are reported without modification, preserving their original scope and any inherent qualifications (e.g., prevalence ranges, \"usually,\" \"may not live beyond\").",
